        "raw": "<p>Hi Playtesters &amp; Christopher,<br />\r\n<br />\r\nI ran into what seemed like a few minor glitches in Ch5 (The Bullpen) I thought I'd share.<br />\r\nThe version I'm referring to is the one shared in the Kickstarter.<br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>1) p. 243<br />\r\n\"Overlord\" Villain Archetype asks you to \"Count the number of <em>minion groups and individual lieutenants</em> ... to determine their current status\", which is defferent from the description of the \"Legion\"&nbsp;archteype (which is sort of the mirror type to this).<br />\r\nHowever, I can't find a definition of minion \"group\" anywhere, and the dice list below refers to the number of (individual?) minions, without mention of Lieutenants, quite&nbsp;like the \"Legion\" archetype. Maybe a leftover from earlier versions?<br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>2) p.261<br />\r\nTypo: The table for temporary abilities allows you, for 4 hero points, to \"emporarily upgrade one&nbsp;of your Yellow abilities\" - missing the T.<br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>3) p. 256-257<br />\r\nNot really a glitch, more of a UX thing: When you want to whip up a twist on-the-fly, the table is not really structed in a very user-friendly way.<br />\r\nAs it is,&nbsp;the type of the twist (add a threat, create a challenge etc.) is the defining&nbsp;top-level argument, while you have to skip across&nbsp;the table to gather the twists&nbsp;that match the current GYRO zone. This certainly seems convenient when&nbsp;writing and balancing the various levels of the same effect type.<br />\r\nBut when <em>playing</em>, you'll usually need quite the opposite structure: The zone is already defined by the situation in play, so you'll look for suitable twist <em>matching that zone</em> (and pick the effect source from there).<br />\r\nTherefore&nbsp;I'd suggest to sort by zone first, and go through the various effect sources on 2nd level within those zones.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Hope this helps!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
